workers/oursharedevents/src/templates/

# npm lockfiles are machine-generated — prettier reformatting them diverges from
# npm's canonical output and produces huge spurious diffs (OSE-790).
package-lock.json
**/package-lock.json

# Generated test-id registry + its Newman bridge. Same reason as the lockfiles
# above: scripts/gen-testids.mjs is the canonical formatter, and its `--check`
# gate is a byte-compare against its own output. Letting prettier reformat them
# makes that gate fail on every run, forever — verified before it reached CI.
web/shared/testids.js
postman/environments/testids.json

# Propagated verbatim from OCR-docs (docs/standards/ -> .ocr-standards/) by the
# propagate-standards workflow. Upstream does not run prettier, so a local reformat
# breaks byte-parity and turns every propagation PR into table-alignment churn
# (observed on OSE-1194). Format them in OCR-docs or not at all.
.ocr-standards/

# Markdown is NOT prettier-owned in this repo, and pretending otherwise is what
# makes reformat noise (OSE-1219). Measured 2026-09-03: 187 of 421 tracked .md
# files are not prettier-clean — 44% — because nothing has ever enforced it.
# No CI job checks markdown formatting: docs-ci runs markdownlint with `|| true`,
# and the lint shard is eslint over config/ scripts/ tests/.
#
# The cost of NOT ignoring these is concrete, not theoretical. Running
# `prettier --write` over four governance files during the 2026-09-03
# reconciliation reflowed 230+ lines that the edit never touched — 294 in
# STORIES.md alone against ~10 real edits — because prettier re-aligns every
# table in the file. That buries the actual change, and it collides with the
# byte-parity the propagated .ocr-standards/ copies need (same failure mode
# already recorded for that directory above).
#
# So: format markdown deliberately or not at all. If markdown formatting is ever
# wanted, it is a one-time repo-wide commit plus a real CI check — not a silent
# side effect of editing one file.
*.md
